    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d65">

                <pb facs="0194" n="102" sameAs="frus:frus1899/pg102"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hay to Count de Lichterveld.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1899-07-31"><hi rendition="#italic">July 31,
                        1899</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 199.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to your note of the 7th instant,
                    asking for permission for the firm of Ingenohl, of Antwerp, to make certain
                    purchases of tobacco at Manila, and for this purpose to charter one or more
                    neutral vessels with which to engage in the coasting trade during the
                    continuance of hostilities, I now have the honor to inform you that it is not
                    deemed advisable by the War Department to grant permission at this time to
                    foreign vessels to engage in the coasting trade in the Philippine Islands.</p>
                <p>Accept, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John Hay.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
